<h3 class=3D"post-title entry-title" itemprop=3D"name">
The speed of the FRAMA (Part 2): The FRASMA
</h3>
<div class=3D"post-header">
<div class=3D"post-header-line-1"></div>
</div>
<div class=3D"post-body entry-content" id=3D"post-body-7305377249981659843"=
 itemprop=3D"description articleBody">
Having explained my preference for a "fractalisation" of the MA to apply on=
 a SMA(rather than on an EMA), I shall now discuss the exact form of this "=
fractalisation".<br>A modification, close to the one recommended by Ehlers,=
 would be to merely divide the period of the SMA by the coefficient =CE=B1,=
 where =CE=B1 is defined as:<br><br><a href=3D"http://www.codecogs.com/eqne=
dit.php?latex=3D%5Cinline%20%5Calpha=3Dexp%28-4.6%28D-1%29%29%5C;%20%5C;%20=
%5C;%20%5C;%20%5C;%20%5C;%20%5C;%20%28E%5C,1%29" target=3D"_blank"><img src=
=3D"http://latex.codecogs.com/gif.latex?%5Cinline%20%5Calpha=3Dexp%28-4.6%2=
8D-1%29%29%5C;%20%5C;%20%5C;%20%5C;%20%5C;%20%5C;%20%5C;%20%28E%5C,1%29" ti=
tle=3D"\inline \alpha=3Dexp(-4.6(D-1))\; \; \; \; \; \; \; (E\,1)"></a><br>=
<br>For a dimension D varying between 1 and 2, such a division would indeed=
 be equivalent to a change of speed in a ratio of 100, the SMA being slowed=
 down 100 times from its initial pace, in the extreme case of a dimension o=
f 2.<br>This dimension D is a numerical approximation of the Box Dimension,=
 itself an approximation of the Hausdorff dimension of the graph, which is =
properly the most mathematically precise fractal dimension. There is howeve=
r, another dimension that can also be seen as a Box Dimension, but of anoth=
er object relating to the process under study, and that Mandelbrot called t=
he Trail Dimension [MAN97,pp.161&amp;172).<br><br>For a Fractional Brownian=
 Motion, we saw earlier that:<br><br><a href=3D"http://www.codecogs.com/eqn=
edit.php?latex=3DD_%7BG%7D=3D2-%5Calpha" target=3D"_blank"><img src=3D"http=
://latex.codecogs.com/gif.latex?D_%7BG%7D=3D2-%5Calpha" title=3D"D_{G}=3D2-=
\alpha"></a><br><br>Where <a href=3D"http://www.codecogs.com/eqnedit.php?la=
tex=3DD_%7BG%7D" target=3D"_blank"><img src=3D"http://latex.codecogs.com/gi=
f.latex?D_%7BG%7D" title=3D"D_{G}"></a> is what we have called so far the F=
ractal dimension, and =CE=B1 is the coefficient of the FBM (which is a diff=
erent thing from the =CE=B1 of equation (E1)) . This latter is actually kno=
wn as the Hurst-Holder exponent (or sometimes as simply the Hurst exponent,=
 in memory of the British hydrologist whose studies of the long-term depend=
ence of the Nile discharges, were inspirational to Mandelbrot works), and m=
ost often designed by H, I used =CE=B1 in reference to Falconer's book, but=
 H seems more convenient from now on. We therefore have:<br><br><a href=3D"=
http://www.codecogs.com/eqnedit.php?latex=3DD_%7BG%7D=3D2-H" target=3D"_bla=
nk"><img src=3D"http://latex.codecogs.com/gif.latex?D_%7BG%7D=3D2-H" title=
=3D"D_{G}=3D2-H"></a><br><br>And <a href=3D"http://www.codecogs.com/eqnedit=
.php?latex=3D%5Cinline%20D_%7BG%7D" target=3D"_blank"><img src=3D"http://la=
tex.codecogs.com/gif.latex?%5Cinline%20D_%7BG%7D" title=3D"\inline D_{G}"><=
/a> will now be known as the Graph Dimension. While the Trail Dimension wil=
l be defined as:<br><br><a href=3D"http://www.codecogs.com/eqnedit.php?late=
x=3D%5Cinline%20D_%7BT%7D=3D1/H" target=3D"_blank"><img src=3D"http://latex=
.codecogs.com/gif.latex?%5Cinline%20D_%7BT%7D=3D1/H" title=3D"\inline D_{T}=
=3D1/H"></a><br>___________________________________________________________=
______________________________________________________<br><br><span style=
=3D"font-weight: bold;">I-Interpretation of the Trail Dimension</span><br><=
br>It is easy to see that the Trail Dimension varies between 1 and =E2=88=
=9E, for the coefficient H varying between 1 and 0. The first question is t=
herefore how a "dimension" growing infinitely should be understood. In [MAN=
97], p.161, Mandelbrot wrote the following explanation:<br><br><blockquote>=
<span style=3D"font-weight: bold;">"First consider a Wiener Brownian motion=
 in the plane. Its coordinates X(t) and Y(t) are independent Brownian motio=
ns. Therefore, if a 1-dimensional Brownian motion X(t) is combined with ano=
ther independent 1-dimensional Brownian motion Y(t), the process X(t) becom=
es "embedded" into a 2-dimensional Brownian motion {X(t),Y(t)}. The value o=
f the trail dimension:<br><a href=3D"http://www.codecogs.com/eqnedit.php?la=
tex=3DD_%7BT%7D=3D2=3D1/H" target=3D"_blank"><img src=3D"http://latex.codec=
ogs.com/gif.latex?D_%7BT%7D=3D2=3D1/H" title=3D"D_{T}=3D2=3D1/H"></a><br>is=
 the fractal dimension of the three dimensional graph of coordinates t,X(t)=
 and Y(t), and the projected "trail" of coordinates X(t) and Y(t). However,=
 the dimension:<br><a href=3D"http://www.codecogs.com/eqnedit.php?latex=3DD=
_%7BG%7D=3D2-H" target=3D"_blank"><img src=3D"http://latex.codecogs.com/gif=
.latex?D_%7BG%7D=3D2-H" title=3D"D_{G}=3D2-H"></a><br>applies to the projec=
ted graphs of coordinates t and X(t) or t and Y(t)."<br></span></blockquote=
><br>My understanding of the above passage, in the general case of FBM (H v=
arying between 0 and 1, while for WBM, H=3D1/2), is that the Trail dimensio=
n must be seen as an approximation of the number of dimensions in which the=
 "real" process takes place (here it might be interesting to understand the=
 term of "dimension" in a data-mining sense, rather than in a strict topolo=
gical sense, prices are clearly the end-result of many independent processe=
s, any of them with the potential of being chaotic in their own right), und=
er the assumption that all the coordinates of the said process can be descr=
ibed as independent Fractional Brownian motions sharing the same Hurst expo=
nent.<br><br><span style=3D"font-weight: bold;">II-Slowing down the MA with=
 the Trail Dimension</span><br><br>It is now possible to conceive of a form=
ula for the coefficient =CE=B1, using the Trail Dimension. The purpose of =
=CE=B1 is to slow down the MA from a reference speed when the Hurst exponen=
t becomes very small, and also to accelerate it when this exponent becomes =
close to 1. The reference speed should be taken as the one used when the pr=
ice varies in a gaussian way, that is when H is 1/2. So for such a value of=
 H, we should have =CE=B1=3D1.<br>If we then consider the following formula=
:<br><br><a href=3D"http://www.codecogs.com/eqnedit.php?latex=3D%5Calpha%20=
=3DD_%7BT%7D/2=3D%5Cfrac%7B1%7D%7B2H%7D%5C;%20%5C;%20%5C;%20%5C;%5C;%20%5C;=
%20%5C;%20%28E2%29" target=3D"_blank"><img src=3D"http://latex.codecogs.com=
/gif.latex?%5Calpha%20=3DD_%7BT%7D/2=3D%5Cfrac%7B1%7D%7B2H%7D%5C;%20%5C;%20=
%5C;%20%5C;%5C;%20%5C;%20%5C;%20%28E2%29" title=3D"\alpha =3DD_{T}/2=3D\fra=
c{1}{2H}\; \; \; \;\; \; \; (E2)"></a><br><br>For a WBM, we have =CE=B1=3D1=
. In addition, for a H tending towards 0, =CE=B1 tends towards infinity, an=
d for H close to 1, =CE=B1=3D1/2.  <br>Comparing =CE=B1 from (E2)(red curve=
) with the inverse of =CE=B1 from (E1)(black curve)(we take the inverse in =
order to get a multiplicative factor rather than a dividing one to apply on=
 the speed of the MA), we get the following graphs:<br><br><a href=3D"https=
://blogger.googleusercontent.com/img/b/R29vZ2xl/AVvXsEiJxU4n4dlyozhdziEe5-7=
RKT1ud1xoJCLV5495YA-P-Lq1wMHHfrYYjWs24bbCEI75X07J7CKSLU-jBlNWG8LxOuCdca2V40=
DvrQMJHgDPNJET3a9TwFCME_8BsqSR38isiS6rlTeYdn0/s1600-h/save.png"><img alt=3D=
"" border=3D"0" id=3D"BLOGGER_PHOTO_ID_5303203260877871106" src=3D"https://=
blogger.googleusercontent.com/img/b/R29vZ2xl/AVvXsEiJxU4n4dlyozhdziEe5-7RKT=
1ud1xoJCLV5495YA-P-Lq1wMHHfrYYjWs24bbCEI75X07J7CKSLU-jBlNWG8LxOuCdca2V40Dvr=
QMJHgDPNJET3a9TwFCME_8BsqSR38isiS6rlTeYdn0/s400/save.png" style=3D"margin: =
0px auto 10px; display: block; text-align: center; cursor: pointer; width: =
400px; height: 240px;"></a><br><br>Or, for a more detailed view of their be=
havior below H=3D1/2:<br><br><a href=3D"https://blogger.googleusercontent.c=
om/img/b/R29vZ2xl/AVvXsEjGs2b6iUcRrHP1nS_M8C2SEf4XNW4eKsMD7Q28yEK61LH4JamcC=
C9h0aMETWvJEIEylX3YCGB8W4ktlsPmnSjY0nPia5uQ8B0cPsgV08mcwnegkIpGxDR4yO2iQ8JB=
2_R9znU-v3u-44w/s1600-h/graph0_0.5_20.png"><img alt=3D"" border=3D"0" id=3D=
"BLOGGER_PHOTO_ID_5303204982702916690" src=3D"https://blogger.googleusercon=
tent.com/img/b/R29vZ2xl/AVvXsEjGs2b6iUcRrHP1nS_M8C2SEf4XNW4eKsMD7Q28yEK61LH=
4JamcCC9h0aMETWvJEIEylX3YCGB8W4ktlsPmnSjY0nPia5uQ8B0cPsgV08mcwnegkIpGxDR4yO=
2iQ8JB2_R9znU-v3u-44w/s400/graph0_0.5_20.png" style=3D"margin: 0px auto 10p=
x; display: block; text-align: center; cursor: pointer; width: 400px; heigh=
t: 240px;"></a><br><br>Dividing the black curve by 10 in order to have an u=
nchanged speed for the case of a WBM, we get the following:<br><br><a href=
=3D"https://blogger.googleusercontent.com/img/b/R29vZ2xl/AVvXsEilT5jRKWZBx1=
WdgScMaYcSZrAx3PVu_qpniBi7lXcAAYay-wMuaCMufUg5ZHKJGRG6FD_9upCSaeVekzWIzWBSt=
G9HSRd35BzJNnATGvRiEBLUcR5lt4rlU0inIJxJ-HzjXHnua1Y5An0/s1600-h/save.png"><i=
mg alt=3D"" border=3D"0" id=3D"BLOGGER_PHOTO_ID_5303207302201143330" src=3D=
"https://blogger.googleusercontent.com/img/b/R29vZ2xl/AVvXsEilT5jRKWZBx1Wdg=
ScMaYcSZrAx3PVu_qpniBi7lXcAAYay-wMuaCMufUg5ZHKJGRG6FD_9upCSaeVekzWIzWBStG9H=
SRd35BzJNnATGvRiEBLUcR5lt4rlU0inIJxJ-HzjXHnua1Y5An0/s400/save.png" style=3D=
"margin: 0px auto 10px; display: block; text-align: center; cursor: pointer=
; width: 400px; height: 240px;"></a><br><br>For H varying from 0.5 to 0, we=
 see that the =CE=B1 coming from (E1) varies almost linearly, for the same =
variation however, we know that the randomness increases in a rather non-li=
near fashion; a linear slowing down of the MA does not seem to reflect this=
 properly. From this theoretical point of view, I therefore prefer the =CE=
=B1 given by equation (E2)(not to mention that it is much more simple).<br>=
<br><span style=3D"font-weight: bold;">III-Implementation of the FRASMA</sp=
an><br><br>I programmed the FRASMA(Fractally modified Simple Moving Average=
) in the MetaTrader platform. You may access and download freely this indic=
ator, as well as use it on the metatrader 4 platform, at <a href=3D"http://=
codebase.mql4.com/5308">this address of the MQL4 Community.</a><br>Please, =
let me know your findings or any criticisms that can improve this indicator=
. <br>Meanwhile, here is a screenshot of three fractally modified MA, the L=
ight Blue is a version of the FRAMA from Ehlers paper (modifying a EMA), th=
e Yellow is a modification of a SMA using the following =CE=B1 inspired by =
Ehlers paper:<br><a href=3D"http://www.codecogs.com/eqnedit.php?latex=3D%5C=
alpha%20=3D1/%2810exp%28-4.6%281-H%29%29%29" target=3D"_blank"><img src=3D"=
http://latex.codecogs.com/gif.latex?%5Calpha%20=3D1/%2810exp%28-4.6%281-H%2=
9%29%29" title=3D"\alpha =3D1/(10exp(-4.6(1-H)))"></a><br>And the Red one i=
s properly the FRASMA, using equation (E2).<br><br><a href=3D"https://blogg=
er.googleusercontent.com/img/b/R29vZ2xl/AVvXsEgby0A0HlTSGSsHJeQtwztH7-bJyV1=
UWGhMVRZAyQSgVaHmPjI9KO-0LDbk6lKSeWBjsCroi2R6ET0UZIOgS7OWHjqKFjKVOz7ytqqx6N=
rYYYQnh9fAuWoJcobGy8Gl5t13xohbOlDt7qI/s1600-h/chart_frasma.gif"><img alt=3D=
"" border=3D"0" id=3D"BLOGGER_PHOTO_ID_5303216062057220242" src=3D"https://=
blogger.googleusercontent.com/img/b/R29vZ2xl/AVvXsEgby0A0HlTSGSsHJeQtwztH7-=
bJyV1UWGhMVRZAyQSgVaHmPjI9KO-0LDbk6lKSeWBjsCroi2R6ET0UZIOgS7OWHjqKFjKVOz7yt=
qqx6NrYYYQnh9fAuWoJcobGy8Gl5t13xohbOlDt7qI/s400/chart_frasma.gif" style=3D"=
margin: 0px auto 10px; display: block; text-align: center; cursor: pointer;=
 width: 400px; height: 243px;"></a><br><br>Below is the fractal Graph Dimen=
sion. The period of reference for all original MA is 20.<br><br><span style=
=3D"font-weight: bold;">IV-Conclusion</span><br><br>My purpose here is not =
to demonstrate that one indicator is better than another, since the quality=
 of an indicator is relative to the manner one uses it. I believe that one =
must be acquainted intuitively with an indicator to use it productively, an=
d it is for this reason that my preference is going to the FRASMA.<br>While=
 one may just rely on direct practise to "understand" at an intuitive level=
 a given indicator, I believe most of us can also profit from a theoretical=
 understanding of them. My goal here is therefore to provide elements along=
 these lines, for others to develop their own familiarity, and maybe provid=
e me, in return, with some of their insights and experiences.<br>It is agai=
n naive to think that a trader, using technical analysis, can actually trad=
e without some level of reliance on his intuition, and it is to totally mis=
s the point of what the fractals tell us about the market to nourish expect=
ations about a deterministic methodology to be successful as a trader, in o=
ther words, there is no Grail to be found in the first place. Nonetheless, =
to understand the technical tools one is using, can improve one's intuition=
, and the overall success of one's trading activity.
